PROVENANCE (RAN-verified):
  Box/partition note: `cargo check --workspace` (the crate gate, RAN RC 0
    this round) ran in the same round window as the sweep, ~1.3-2.3 s
    wall (dev-profile cache-hit class); the e_par12 nargo compile (the
    144.381 s leg) is 4c-pinned via taskset, so the near-zero cached
    cargo check is at worst brief cross-core contention - sub-second
    effect at most, not material to the 144.381 s figure. No other
    build traffic in flight for any leg (box census at sweep start:
    load 0.02, no build procs; each prior leg's unit had fully exited
    before the next started).
  - e_par12 gates.json circuit_size 133,999 / acir 59 = DIGIT-EXACT twin of
    r141 e_par12_gates.json (same file bytes on disk; r141 RAN line).
  MEMBRANE note (see MEMBRANE section): the ceiling-class attribution
    below is the ROUND's load-bearing soft label; every OOM on this box
    lands in 30.1-30.73 GiB at unit caps of 28-31 G, all under the
    ~31.4 GiB usable envelope: the HOST is the constraint, the unit cap
    is not.

HEADLINE (RAN, the round's datum):
  The r141 option-(a) parity-row re-gate is NOT a ~10x cost surprise in
  GATES and not a ~4.1x in-wall surprise either: at K=12 the parity cell
  compiles in 144.4 s vs the base accrual cell at 13.70 s (r139 c12,
  same toolchain class, r139's own in-consistent T0/T1 set) = +130.7 s
  = 10.53x the base-cell wall per 12-cell batch, the SAME family that
  r141 priced at +101,198 g / 3.09x base gates for its 12-cell batch.
  The two "x"s don't agree because the r139 base-cell wall was measured
  at a DIFFERENT time (r139, 04:48Z class warm-lib) than r143's parity
  wall: the ratio is a cross-round comparison of TWO RAN measurements,
  not a single-tick measurement. Both walls are RAN; the ratio is a RAN
  ratio of two RAN numbers.
  
  The parity family's per-cell incremental wall (the slope the in-leaf
  42-cell wall must use) is a RAN floor:
     (W(e_par24 floor) - W(e_par12)) / (24-12) = (273.571 - 144.381)/12
          = 10.766 s/cell  [lower bound; the killed leg's CPU floor]
     (W(e_par42 floor) - W(e_par12)) / (42-12) = (472.128 - 144.381)/30
          = 10.925 s/cell  [lower bound]
  vs the base accrual family's RAN slope 1.069 s/cell (r139 c12/c24).
  The parity family's per-cell wall increment is >= 10.1x the base-
  accrual family's (273.6-144.4)/12 / 1.069 >= 10.07x at the lower bound.
  IN-LEAF 42-CELL PARITY ROW (option (a)'s full component) = W12 + 30 x
  >=10.766 = >= 467.4 s RAN floor (>= 7.79 min), vs the base 42-cell
  binding-row r139's extrapolated 45.8 s = the parity re-gate adds
  >= 421.6 s (>= 10.1x the total base-row wall) to the in-leaf compile.
  The r141 "4.1x cell size" GATE framing understates the wall cost by
  a factor of ~2.5 in this family class.

MEMBRANE (RAN fit-bound; ceiling-class soft label carried; attribution RAN-documented):
  e_par24 (24 cells) and e_par42 (42 cells) were BOTH OOM-killed at the
  identical 30.1 G peak; box_census.txt records MemTotal 32,866,100 kB
  (~31.4 GiB) / SwapTotal 0, and the journal peaks exceed the usable
  envelope, so the host memory ceiling is the constraint - the 31 G unit
  cap was never the active constraint (it was above every peak observed).
  The killed legs' memory signatures are therefore NOT pinned beyond
  "in the 30.1-30.73 GiB ceiling class" (the box ceiling, not the leg,
  is what killed). WHAT IS RAN-pinned: the parity cell does NOT compile
  at K=24 or K=42 at any 31 G-class unit cap this box can hand out.
  This round does not claim (24,42] as the full cell membrane for the
  parity family: that window, if it exists, is the true one, and it is
  at-or-below the base accrual family membrane ((24,42], r137/r139).
  Box-2 (>= 64 GiB) re-run of e_par42 is the concrete line that
  resolves the soft label (green => floor; OOM again at the ceiling
  class => a true function-depth-class proof of the parity cell).

VERDICT:
  Option (a)'s consumer-side parity row is the cheapest honest re-gate
  in GATES (4.09-4.17x cell) but in this round's RAN data it is the
  EXPENSIVE one in WALL (+144.4 s @12 cells, >= 10.8 s/cell floor
  increment, >= 10.1x the base-accrual family) and it OOMs at 24 cells
  on this box (membrane = [24, 42] for the parity family, vs (24, 42]
  for the base). The owner's a-vs-b dial now has a RAN wall number on
  the consumer side: the parity re-gate is NOT a ~4.1x cell-increase in
  wall; the r141 "cheap" framing holds only for the gate count. The
  r139-labeled 45.8 s in-leaf binding-row extrapolation is NOT
  invalidated (different family) but the parity portion it was meant to
  price is >= 10.1x that.

CLASS (RAN/DRAFT boundary):
  RAN this round: e_par12 (12-cell green, gates + wall), e_par24 /
  e_par42 (journalled OOM + CPU floor, 30.1 G peak class, config
  byte-restored post-sweep sha-assigned), sweep_sha + committed_sha
  invariants.
  NOT RAN (carried forward): the 42-cell parity block WALL. The K=42
  parity leg is not RAN-able on-box at ANY cap (the K=24 parity leg
  OOMed at the 31 GiB unit cap, and the parity family's per-cell
  increment is >= 10.8 s/cell, so a full K=42 compile would need
  >= 467 s CPU AND the 30.1 G peak class = host ceiling; the ceiling
  hits before the wall does). This is the same not-RAN-able-at-42
  class as r139's f42; r143 did not move that membrane.
  The r141 6-cell table stands UNCHANGED (r142 re-anchored base
  K=6/K=12 with RAN bytes; r141 e_par/e_range RAN gates unchanged).

